<ContentLayout title="Instant HTML Audit: What You Can Check Without a Crawl" description="Fast HTML checks provide observations from supplied source. Learn which results are immediate and which need a live crawl." breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} publishDate="2026-03-28" updateDate="2026-09-05" showCTA={false}>
<h2>Instant Results Need a Defined Scope</h2><p>A pasted-source check can identify a missing title or a noindex tag immediately because the relevant markup is already available. It cannot know how fast a remote server responds or whether Google has indexed a page. Speed of output is not evidence of audit depth.</p>
<p><a href="/free-website-audit/">Open the free HTML SEO checker</a> to inspect your page source.</p>
<h2>Run and Verify a Small Check</h2><p>Copy public page source, supply its page URL and run the HTML checker. Compare each finding with the source. Change one element and rerun to verify that the observation follows the input. Use the included example to see a noindex directive and a missing alt attribute.</p>
<h2>Follow Up Outside the Tool</h2><p>Use browser testing for forms and mobile layout. Use Search Console for Google indexing and search data. Use performance measurements for page speed. Record the source, scope and date beside every result. No single instant score replaces these checks.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/resources/agency-audit-toolkit/">editable agency toolkit</a> to record evidence and implementation steps. The <a href="/website-audit-checklist/template/">50-check PDF</a> covers a wider manual review.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/case-studies/auditweb-self-audit/">documented self-audit</a> provides real examples of observations and corrections. Review the <a href="/website-audit-services/">professional audit scope</a> if the problem requires evidence beyond your page source.</p>
</ContentLayout>
